# EXERCISE 6: TASK SCHEDULING AND LOAD BALANCING
# PART 1 - TASK SCHEDULING WITH CRON
# 1. Start the Ubuntu VM (the manual gives UbuntuSelcia as an example).
# 2. Create the script:
#    nano ~/script.sh
# 3. Put the following content in script.sh:
#    #!/bin/bash
#    echo "Hello Selcia"
# 4. Save (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X) and make it executable:
#    chmod +x ~/script.sh
# 5. Open your crontab:
#    crontab -e
# 6. Add this line to run the script every minute and append output to a log:
#    * * * * * /bin/bash /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/script.sh >> /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/output.log 2>&1
#    Replace <YOUR_USERNAME> with the result of: whoami
# 7. Check output:
#    cat ~/output.log
#    Check cron logs (the manual suggests): tail -f /var/log/syslog | grep CRON
#
# PART 2 - HAPROXY LOAD BALANCING
# 1. Install: sudo apt update && sudo apt install haproxy -y
# 2. Edit: sudo nano /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
# 3. Configure frontend/backend, replacing the IP placeholders:
#    frontend http_front
#       bind *:80
#       default_backend http_back
#
#    backend http_back
#       balance roundrobin
#       server vm1 <VM1_IP>:80 check
#       server vm2 <VM2_IP>:80 check
#       server vm3 <VM3_IP>:80 check
# 4. Validate/restart/enable/check:
#    sudo haproxy -c -f /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
#    sudo systemctl restart haproxy
#    sudo systemctl enable haproxy
#    sudo systemctl status haproxy
# 5. Browse to http://<LOAD_BALANCER_IP>. The manual example is
#    http://192.168.119.128, but use the address of your own load-balancer VM.
#
# PART 3 - OPTIONAL ADVANCED SCHEDULING AND PRIORITIES
# 1. Create two scripts with different workloads, e.g. script1.sh and script2.sh.
# 2. Make each executable: chmod +x ~/script1.sh ~/script2.sh
# 3. Open crontab: crontab -e
# 4. Example jobs with different nice priorities (update paths/usernames):
#    * * * * * /usr/bin/nice -n 10 /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/script1.sh >> /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/script1.log 2>&1
#    * * * * * /usr/bin/nice -n 0 /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/script2.sh >> /home/<YOUR_USERNAME>/script2.log 2>&1
#    nice values range from -20 (higher priority) to 19 (lower priority).
# RESULT: Cron automates jobs; HAProxy distributes HTTP requests.

